# What is Spring Boot?

## Introduction

Spring Boot is an extension of the Spring Framework that simplifies Java application development.

It helps developers build production-ready applications quickly by reducing configuration effort and boilerplate code.

Spring Boot is built on top of Spring Framework and provides:

* Auto Configuration
* Starter Dependencies
* Embedded Servers
* Production-Ready Features
* Opinionated Defaults

As a result, developers can focus more on business logic and less on infrastructure setup.

---

## Evolution of Spring

```text
Java EE
   ↓
Spring Framework
   ↓
Spring Boot
```

### Java EE

* Complex configuration
* XML-heavy setup
* Difficult maintenance

### Spring Framework

* Dependency Injection
* IoC Container
* Better modularity

### Spring Boot

* Auto Configuration
* Embedded Servers
* Faster development
* Minimal setup

Spring Boot was introduced to make Spring development even easier.

---

## Why Was Spring Boot Created?

Although Spring Framework significantly improved Java development, developers still faced several challenges:

* Large amounts of XML configuration
* Complex project setup
* Manual dependency management
* External server configuration
* Boilerplate code

As applications grew larger, setting up a new Spring project could become time-consuming.

Spring Boot was introduced to solve these problems and improve developer productivity.

---

## What Problems Does Spring Boot Solve?

### Before Spring Boot

Developers typically needed to:

* Configure XML files manually
* Set up Tomcat or other servers
* Configure DispatcherServlet
* Configure JSON converters
* Manage dependencies individually
* Write additional configuration classes

This increased complexity and development time.

---

### With Spring Boot

Spring Boot provides:

* Auto Configuration
* Embedded Servers
* Starter Dependencies
* Production-Ready Features
* Opinionated Defaults

This allows developers to start building features almost immediately.

---

