## Key Features of Spring Boot

### 1. Auto Configuration

Spring Boot automatically configures application components based on dependencies available in the classpath.

For example:

If you add:

```xml
<dependency>
    <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
    <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-web</artifactId>
</dependency>
```

Spring Boot automatically configures:

* Embedded Tomcat
* DispatcherServlet
* Jackson JSON Support

without requiring manual setup.

### Benefits

* Less Configuration
* Faster Development
* Reduced Boilerplate Code

---

### 2. Embedded Server

Spring Boot applications contain an embedded web server.

Supported servers:

* Tomcat (Default)
* Jetty
* Undertow

This eliminates the need to deploy WAR files to external servers.

Developers can simply run the application using:

```bash
mvn spring-boot:run
```

or directly from their IDE.

---

### 3. Starter Dependencies

Starter dependencies simplify dependency management.

Example:

```xml
<dependency>
    <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
    <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-web</artifactId>
</dependency>
```

This single dependency includes everything required to build REST APIs.

Other common starters include:

* spring-boot-starter-data-jpa
* spring-boot-starter-security
* spring-boot-starter-test

---

### 4. Production-Ready Features

Spring Boot provides production-ready capabilities through Spring Boot Actuator.

Examples:

* Health Checks
* Metrics
* Monitoring
* Application Information

These features help monitor applications in production environments.

---

### 5. Faster Development

Developers spend less time configuring applications and more time implementing business features.

This leads to:

* Faster Delivery
* Improved Productivity
* Easier Maintenance

---

## Basic Spring Boot Application

```java
@SpringBootApplication
public class Application {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        SpringApplication.run(Application.class, args);
    }
}
```

The application starts an embedded server and initializes the Spring Container automatically.

---

## Understanding @SpringBootApplication

The `@SpringBootApplication` annotation combines:

```java
@Configuration
@EnableAutoConfiguration
@ComponentScan
```

### @Configuration

Marks the class as a source of bean definitions.

### @EnableAutoConfiguration

Enables Spring Boot's auto-configuration mechanism.

### @ComponentScan

Scans the project for Spring components such as:

* @Controller
* @Service
* @Repository
* @Component

---

## Spring Framework vs Spring Boot

| Spring Framework         | Spring Boot           |
| ------------------------ | --------------------- |
| More Configuration       | Minimal Configuration |
| Manual Setup             | Auto Configuration    |
| External Server Required | Embedded Server       |
| More Boilerplate Code    | Less Boilerplate Code |
| Slower Project Setup     | Faster Project Setup  |

---

## Real-World Example

Suppose you want to create a Product Management REST API.

### Without Spring Boot

You need to:

* Configure Tomcat
* Configure DispatcherServlet
* Configure JSON converters
* Configure dependencies manually

### With Spring Boot

You simply:

1. Add Spring Web Starter
2. Create a Controller
3. Run the Application

The REST API can be running within minutes.

